sequenceDiagram
  %% Target design (agreed with Nick, 2026-11-07): a task VM and its identity are disposable; the release frees its place. Orchestrator 2026-11-08: keeping approved work is a precondition of the cancel, not a step of it.
  %% Orchestrator 2026-11-13: a task copy ends through its Instance destroy; the cancel records the copy's unpushed work and does not block on it.
  %% status: draft
  %% command: task:cancel
  %% kind: tasks.cancel
  autonumber
  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue
  %% lane GH icon=server pill=target tone=purple
  %% lane SW icon=workflow pill=executes tone=green
  %% lane H icon=server pill=target tone=amber
  %% lane HUB icon=server pill=target tone=purple
  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API
  participant GH as GitHub<br/>repository
  participant SW as Swamp<br/>serve on Node 209
  participant H as Task VM host<br/>Incus over ssh
  participant HUB as VPN hub<br/>over ssh
  Note over GW: Check before any change<br/>VM free · cancellable
  %%> Requirements:
  %%> - no Operation is open on the group's task VM, such as a resume or an earlier release; otherwise HTTP 409 `operation.target_busy`, naming it
  %%> - the group is cancellable; otherwise `tasks.not_cancellable`
  %%> - after the group's running work is asked to stop, no Operation still holds the group, such as a timed-out one whose run nothing has proven ended; otherwise HTTP 409 `operation.target_busy`, naming it
  %%> - every refusal here and in the next step comes before the cancel stops running work, so a refused cancel changes nothing
  GW->>GH: Compare the task branch<br/>approved...task-42
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - resolves the group's latest approved commit: the latest commit Orbit reviewed (ADR 0203), or else the commit of the latest approval
  %%> - reads origin's task branch head and compares it with that commit, with a `contents: read` token
  %%> - only when GitHub answers that the branch does not exist (404) and the group's pull request merged does that pull request's head stand in; the pull request is read with a `pull_requests: read` token
  %%> - skipped when the group has no approval, or holds no workspace and no task VM
  GH-->>GW: Branch reaches it
  %%> Result:
  %%> - otherwise the cancel refuses with HTTP 409 `tasks.approved_work_unpublished`, names the commit and the branch, and admits nothing: run `task:publish` first, which resumes a parked VM
  %%> - every other outcome refuses the same way: a GitHub read that fails, a branch that is gone without a merged pull request
  alt the task owns a task copy
    Note over GW: Read the copy's workspace<br/>one bounded read
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - one SSH call to the copy's Node, bounded at 4 s, reads what the copy holds beyond its seed: commits that origin lacks, uncommitted, untracked and stashed files
    %%> - a stopped or unreachable copy reads `unknown` with its reason; the cancel goes on
    Note over GW: Admit the copy's destroy<br/>instance.destroy · force
    %%> Requirements:
    %%> - no Operation holds the copy, such as its create or an earlier destroy; otherwise HTTP 409 `operation.target_busy`, naming it
    %%> Result:
    %%> - the cancel is recorded as succeeded at once (R1), right after the destroy's admission; its result names the destroy Operation and holds the workspace read
    %%> - the destroy runs as every Instance destroy does; when it succeeds, a task that still runs and its open subtasks are `cancelled`, and the task lets go of the copy; a `failed` task stays `failed`
    %%> - when it fails, the task runs on and keeps the copy; the next cancel admits the destroy again
  else no VM and no workspace
    Note over GW: End the task at admission<br/>R1: no run
    %%> When:
    %%> - the task holds no task VM and no workspace, such as a `todo` task or one that waits for a place; its waiting Operation is cancelled first
    %%> Result:
    %%> - in the admission transaction, the task and its open subtasks are `cancelled` and the Operation is recorded as succeeded; no Swamp run starts (R1)
    %%> - the HTTP 202 already carries the succeeded Operation, so `task:show` reads `cancelled` from then on
  else the task holds a VM
    Note over GW: Admit the release<br/>stop work · VM reserved
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - cancellation takes precedence over the group's capacity wait or running task; cancel that task before acquiring its group lock for cleanup
    %%> - stops the group's running work, then, in one transaction, locks and re-reads the VM, refuses if an Operation opened on it meanwhile, and marks it `releasing`, so no later resume starts it
    %%> Result:
    %%> - the run carries only the release; it pushes nothing and never starts a parked VM
    %%> - a start that Swamp refuses fails the cancel and marks the VM `failed`, so the reservation goes and another cancel reserves it again
    GW->>SW: Start run, full intent<br/>operation-task-cancel
    SW->>H: Delete the VM<br/>and its seed clone
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - one host phase: `incus delete --force`, the seed clone and the identity volume; a few seconds
    SW->>HUB: Remove the hub peer<br/>wg set remove · peer file
    %%> Runs beside the step before it: Swamp starts both at once.
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - the VM's peer leaves the WireGuard hub; the Pi token goes with the VM. There is no model key to revoke: the fixed key stays with the guard, for every other VM
    SW-->>GW: Run succeeded<br/>place free
    Note over GW: Delete the Node record<br/>target under 10 s
    %%> Result:
    %%> - the Node, its role and its Process go; the place is free for the next task start
  end
